<%# The classroom form, for both #new and #edit.

    Rebuilt. What was wrong with it, measured rather than asserted:

    - **Five elements shared `id="classroom_grade_ids_"`** - the hidden field and all four grade
      checkboxes - because `check_box_tag "classroom[grade_ids][]"` derives one id from the name. Invalid
      HTML, and any `for=` or `aria-*` reference resolves to whichever came first.
    - **The "Grades" label labelled nothing.** `form.label :grade_ids` emits `for="classroom_grade_ids"`,
      and no element has that id - the checkboxes end in an underscore. The group had no accessible name
      at all. It is a `<fieldset>` with a `<legend>` now, which is what a group of checkboxes is, and what
      design.md expects for a hand-placed group.
    - **Both option lists were `max-h-60 overflow-y-auto` boxes with a slate-50 fill** around content that
      trivially fits: four grades and, in this database, one teacher. A scroll container that can never
      scroll, and a tinted panel inside what is now a card - the same "conditional affordance with no
      condition" shape recorded elsewhere here.
    - **Two HTML comments** shipped to the browser. A view comment belongs in an ERB comment tag, which
      the server strips; an HTML one is sent and the reader can read it.
    - The form sat on **no surface** while students#new and #edit and every admin form use `.tw-card`.
    - `my-5` on each group, so the first and last field carried outer margin the card then doubled.

    School, year and the teacher assignment are admin-only, matching ClassroomPolicy's permitted
    attributes. A teacher who saw these would be filling in fields whose values are dropped on submit,
    which is worse than not offering them: the page would look like it saved and quietly would not. The
    check is the same policy the parameter filter uses, so the two cannot drift apart. %>
<%# **One classroom form, for both halves of the product.**

    `/classrooms/new` and `/admin/classrooms/new` were two different forms for one model, and they had
    drifted into two different products: this one asks for a school and a year and offers the teacher
    assignment; the admin one asked for a single school year, offered no teachers, wrapped every field in a
    `mb-6` and led with an `h3` restating the page title. An admin creating a classroom got different
    fields depending on which URL they came in through.

    They render the same partial now, so the two cannot diverge again. What varies is what has to vary: the
    URL it posts to and where Cancel goes. Everything else - which fields exist, in what order, with what
    validation - is one decision made once. %>
<% admin_fields = policy(classroom).permitted_attributes.include?(:school_id) %>
<% url = local_assigns.fetch(:url, nil) %>
<% cancel_path = local_assigns.fetch(:cancel_path) { classroom.persisted? ? classroom : classrooms_path } %>

<%= form_with(model: classroom, url: url, class: "contents") do |form| %>
  <%= render "shared/form_errors", record: classroom, subject: "classroom" %>

  <%# No `mt-*`. It carried `mt-4`, which did nothing in the ordinary case: adjacent vertical margins collapse
      to the larger, and the page header's own `mb-6` is bigger - measured, the gap was 24px with it and 24px
      without. An inert declaration is worse than none, because it reads as load-bearing and it *became*
      load-bearing the moment an error summary appeared between the two, giving that state a 16px gap where
      the rest of the page uses 24. The summary spaces itself now. %>
  <div class="tw-card p-5">
    <div class="space-y-5">
      <div>
        <%= form.label :name, class: "tw-label-primary" do %>
          Name<span class="required-indicator">*</span>
        <% end %>
        <%= form.text_field :name, class: "tw-input-primary", required: true %>
      </div>

      <% if admin_fields %>
        <%# Held in a local because a three-link safe-navigation chain is one link past what rubocop
            allows, and both selects need it. %>
        <% school_year = classroom&.school_year %>

        <div>
          <%= form.label :school_id, "School name", class: "tw-label-primary" %>
          <% selected_school = school_year&.school&.id %>
          <%= form.select(
                :school_id,
                options_from_collection_for_select(@schools, :id, :name, selected_school),
                { prompt: "Select a school" },
                { class: "tw-input-primary" }
              ) %>
        </div>

        <div>
          <%= form.label :year_id, "Year", class: "tw-label-primary" %>
          <% selected_year = school_year&.year&.id %>
          <%= form.select(
                :year_id,
                options_from_collection_for_select(@years, :id, :name, selected_year),
                { prompt: "Select a year" },
                { class: "tw-input-primary" }
              ) %>
        </div>
      <% end %>

      <%# A group of checkboxes is a fieldset, and its legend is the group's name - the one thing a
          `<label for>` cannot be, since there is no single control to point at. GOV.UK, Polaris and
          Primer all use a fieldset here.

          `mb-2` on the legend rather than `mt-*` on what follows: a legend's default rendering already
          sits it on the border box, and design.md records that a fieldset's inline margin needs resetting
          rather than compensating for. %>
      <%# The group's error goes on the fieldset, not on whichever checkbox Rails renders first. The
          field_error_proc handles every text-like control automatically and skips checkboxes for exactly
          this reason - see FormErrorsHelper. %>
      <%= tag.fieldset(**field_error_attrs(classroom, :grades)) do %>
        <legend class="tw-label-primary">
          Grades<span class="required-indicator">*</span>
        </legend>

        <%# **Under the subheader, before the options.** It sat below the grid, which put the instruction
            after the decision it governs and left it floating between this group and the next - design.md
            says hint text goes "under the group's subheader ... not floating between two field groups",
            because a hint between groups is ambiguous about which one it modifies. GOV.UK puts a fieldset's
            hint between the legend and the inputs for the same reason.

            It used to open with a red "* Required:", which was a third statement of one fact - the asterisk
            on the legend and the browser's own validation being the other two - in the colour this app
            reserves for errors. %>
        <p class="mt-1 mb-3 text-sm text-slate-600">Select at least one grade that applies to this classroom.</p>

        <%# One empty value, so unchecking everything submits an empty list rather than omitting the key
            and leaving the old grades in place. `id: nil` because it would otherwise take the same
            derived id as every checkbox below it. %>
        <%= hidden_field_tag "classroom[grade_ids][]", "", id: nil %>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-1 lg:grid-cols-2">
          <% Grade.where(level: Classroom::GRADE_RANGE).order(:level).each do |grade| %>
            <%# `py-2` for the row's height and its hover band, and **no `px-2`**: horizontal padding here
                pushed the checkbox 8px right of the form's left edge, so the grades and teachers boxes sat
                at 518 while the inputs above and the trading checkbox below sat at 510. Reported as the
                teacher checkbox looking misaligned, and it was - against the form's gutter, not against its
                own label.

                Every control in a form shares one left edge; GOV.UK's checkbox item has no left padding for
                exactly this reason, and an indent is reserved for a nested or dependent option. The
                alternative - keeping the padding and pulling the row back with a negative margin - is the
                thing this codebase has recorded three times as the wrong fix, because the hover fill goes
                with it. %>
            <label class="flex items-center gap-3 rounded-lg py-2 transition-colors hover:bg-slate-50">
              <%= check_box_tag "classroom[grade_ids][]",
                                grade.id,
                                classroom.persisted? && classroom.grades.include?(grade),
                                id: "classroom_grade_#{grade.id}",
                                class: "size-4 shrink-0 rounded-sm border-slate-300 accent-sitf-primary " \
                                       "focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 " \
                                       "focus-visible:outline-sitf-primary" %>
              <span class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900"><%= grade.name %></span>
            </label>
          <% end %>
        </div>

        <%= field_error(classroom, :grades) %>
      <% end %>

      <% if admin_fields %>
        <%# Checkboxes, and no avatars.

            **The control is right for the volume.** A checkbox list is what GOV.UK, Polaris and Primer all
            use for a short, fully known set of options, and there is one teacher in this database. Beyond
            roughly ten it should become a searchable multi-select with chips - GitHub's assignees picker,
            Linear's, Jira's - because a list you have to scroll to find a name in is a list you should be
            able to type into. That threshold is the trigger, not a redesign for its own sake.

            **The avatar is gone.** An avatar earns its place by making a person recognisable at a glance,
            and a 32px disc containing one letter does not: two teachers whose names begin with T get the
            same disc, so it identified nobody while taking the widest column in the row. The name and the
            email do the identifying, and the email is what disambiguates. Reported as not making sense,
            which it did not.

            The email is the secondary line under the name - design.md's primary-identifier shape, and what
            GitHub's collaborator lists and Google Workspace's member pickers show for exactly this. %>
        <%= tag.fieldset(**field_error_attrs(classroom, :teachers)) do %>
          <legend class="tw-label-primary">Teachers</legend>

          <%# Under the subheader, before the options, like the grades hint. %>
          <p class="mt-1 mb-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
            Teachers you select can see this classroom and edit its grades and trading setting.
          </p>

          <%= hidden_field_tag "classroom[teacher_ids][]", "", id: nil %>

          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-1">
            <% @teachers.each do |teacher| %>
              <label class="flex items-start gap-3 rounded-lg py-2 transition-colors hover:bg-slate-50">
                <%# mt-0.5 on a 16px box beside a two-line block, so the checkbox's centre lands on the
                    name's first line rather than its top edge - design.md states that for a lone checkbox
                    and measures it as centre-to-centre. %>
                <%= check_box_tag "classroom[teacher_ids][]",
                                  teacher.id,
                                  classroom.persisted? && classroom.teachers.include?(teacher),
                                  id: "classroom_teacher_ids_#{teacher.id}",
                                  class: "mt-0.5 size-4 shrink-0 rounded-sm border-slate-300 " \
                                         "accent-sitf-primary focus-visible:outline-2 " \
                                         "focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-sitf-primary" %>

                <span class="min-w-0">
                  <span class="block truncate text-sm font-medium text-slate-900"><%= teacher.display_name %></span>
                  <span class="block truncate text-xs text-slate-600"><%= teacher.email %></span>
                </span>
              </label>
            <% end %>
          </div>

          <%= field_error(classroom, :teachers) %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>

      <%# **Last in the form**, after what the classroom *is* and who can see it.

          It is the only field here with a runtime effect on students, it is optional with a default, and it
          is the one setting that also has its own control on the classroom page - so in a form it is "and
          one more thing", not part of the classroom's identity. Required identity first, access next,
          behaviour last is the order GOV.UK and Polaris both use when fields are grouped on one page.

          A single checkbox is the right control and not a switch: GOV.UK reserves a lone checkbox for
          opting in, which is what this is, and a switch implies the change takes effect as you flip it -
          which it does on the classroom page, and does not here, where nothing happens until you save.

          The hint is indented to the label's text column rather than starting under the checkbox, so it
          reads as belonging to that option. GOV.UK indents a checkbox's hint the same way. `pl-7` is the
          16px box plus the 12px gap. %>
      <div>
        <label class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <%= form.check_box :trading_enabled,
                             class: "size-4 shrink-0 rounded-sm border-slate-300 accent-sitf-primary " \
                                    "focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 " \
                                    "focus-visible:outline-sitf-primary" %>
          <span class="tw-label-primary mb-0">Enable trading</span>
        </label>
        <p class="mt-1 pl-7 text-sm text-slate-600">
          When enabled, students in this classroom can place buy and sell orders.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# Leading edge, primary first, on the page background below the card - design.md's form-actions rule.
      The submit label is explicit: a bare `form.submit` lets Rails generate "Create Classroom" from the
      model name, which is Title Case and invisible to any copy sweep, since the string exists nowhere in
      the source.

      Cancel replaces the pair of `tw-btn-secondary` links that used to sit under the form - "View
      classroom" and "Back to classrooms" - which put three actions on the page in two groups with the
      primary orphaned above them. Going back is one action, and on #edit it goes where Cancel goes. %>
  <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
    <%= form.submit classroom.persisted? ? "Update classroom" : "Create classroom", class: "tw-btn-primary" %>
    <%= link_to "Cancel", cancel_path, class: "tw-btn-secondary" %>
  </div>
<% end %>
